<!--
  arm.xml — a humanoid upper body, welded to the world, written for Synapsa Commons lesson
  F15-L02 (kinematics, frames and Jacobians).

  Authored for this lesson by the Synapsa Commons project and released under CC0-1.0
  (https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/). Nothing is downloaded: the file
  ships inside the lesson directory and the lesson never touches the network.

  WHY THIS MODEL

  The lesson's subject is the map from joint angles to a point in space, and its derivative.
  Three properties were chosen deliberately to make that map legible.

  1. EVERY JOINT IS A HINGE, and the pelvis is welded to the world with no free joint. So
     nq == nv == 10, and qpos index i is dof index i for every i. That 1:1 mapping is what
     lets a student finite-difference the forward kinematics by writing qpos[i] += eps
     directly. On a model with a free or ball joint the mapping breaks — qpos carries a
     4-number quaternion where qvel carries a 3-number angular velocity — and the shortcut
     silently produces a wrong Jacobian. The lesson says so, and section 9 loads a
     short model with a ball joint to show nq != nv rather than assert it.

  2. THE TWO ARMS ARE DELIBERATELY ASYMMETRIC. The right arm has six joints (three at the
     shoulder, an elbow, two at the wrist) and, with the two waist joints, eight degrees of
     freedom move the right palm. The left arm has two joints that move it not at all. Their
     Jacobian columns are therefore EXACTLY zero, not nearly zero, which is the cleanest
     possible demonstration that a Jacobian column answers "if this one dof moved, where
     would this point go?" — and it gives the finite-difference exercise an honest reason to
     difference a handful of dofs rather than all of them.

  3. THE HOME POSE IS SINGULAR ON PURPOSE. At qpos = 0 the whole right arm hangs straight
     down, fully extended: shoulder origin at z = 1.38, palm at z = 0.745, so the palm is
     exactly 0.635 m = 0.28 + 0.25 + 0.105 below the shoulder, the sum of the link lengths.
     A fully extended arm cannot move its palm along its own axis at any joint velocity, so
     the translational Jacobian loses rank there — its smallest singular value is exactly
     zero, and the undamped normal equations are not merely ill-conditioned but singular.
     The inverse-kinematics section starts every solve from this pose, which is what makes
     the damping term load-bearing rather than decorative: with lambda = 0 the solve raises
     LinAlgError on its first iteration. The notebook measures that; it does not claim it.

  Contacts are disabled. Nothing in this lesson touches anything, and a contact-free
  mj_forward on ten dofs costs a few microseconds, which is what puts a damping sweep, a
  step-size sweep and a hundred inverse-kinematics iterations inside a laptop CPU budget.

  GEOMETRY (x forward, y left, z up; pelvis welded at z = 0.90)

      pelvis     no joint                       welded to the world
      torso      waist_yaw, waist_pitch         2 dofs
      right arm  shoulder pitch/roll/yaw,       6 dofs, ending in the "palm" site
                 elbow, wrist pitch/roll
      left arm   shoulder pitch, elbow          2 dofs, and they move the right palm not at all

  Generalised coordinates (model.nq == model.nv == 10), in order:
      0 waist_yaw        1 waist_pitch
      2 shoulder_r_pitch 3 shoulder_r_roll  4 shoulder_r_yaw
      5 elbow_r          6 wrist_r_pitch    7 wrist_r_roll
      8 shoulder_l_pitch 9 elbow_l

  There are no actuators. This lesson never steps time: it sets qpos, runs forward
  kinematics, and reads positions and Jacobians. Adding motors would invite a student to
  step the model and wonder why the arm sags, which is the next lesson's subject.

  THE PALM SITE

  "palm" sits 0.105 m beyond the hand body's frame origin, along the hand's own -z. That
  offset is the point of the frames section: the hand body origin, the hand body's centre of
  mass and the palm site are three different points rigidly attached to the same body, so
  they have three different translational Jacobians even though the body — and therefore
  every joint axis in the chain — is identical. mj_jacBody, mj_jacBodyCom and mj_jacSite are
  the same computation at three different points, and the notebook measures the gap between
  them rather than describing it.
-->
<mujoco model="f15l02_upper_body">
  <compiler angle="radian" autolimits="true"/>

  <option timestep="0.002" gravity="0 0 -9.81">
    <!-- Nothing in this task touches anything. -->
    <flag contact="disable"/>
  </option>

  <visual>
    <rgba haze="0.15 0.25 0.35 1"/>
    <global elevation="-15" azimuth="130"/>
  </visual>

  <default>
    <geom type="capsule" contype="0" conaffinity="0" rgba="0.72 0.74 0.80 1"/>
    <joint type="hinge" armature="0.01" damping="0.5"/>
  </default>

  <worldbody>
    <light pos="0 0 3" dir="0 0 -1" diffuse="0.8 0.8 0.8"/>
    <geom name="floor" type="plane" size="3 3 0.1" pos="0 0 0" rgba="0.26 0.28 0.33 1"
          contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>

    <!-- No joint on the pelvis: it is welded to the world, so every dof below is a named
         joint and nq == nv. -->
    <body name="pelvis" pos="0 0 0.90">
      <geom name="pelvis_geom" fromto="0 0 0 0 0 0.12" size="0.10" mass="12"/>

      <body name="torso" pos="0 0 0.12">
        <joint name="waist_yaw"   axis="0 0 1" range="-1.2 1.2"/>
        <joint name="waist_pitch" axis="0 1 0" range="-0.7 0.7"/>
        <geom name="torso_geom" fromto="0 0 0 0 0 0.40" size="0.09" mass="18"/>

        <body name="upper_arm_right" pos="0 -0.19 0.36">
          <joint name="shoulder_r_pitch" axis="0 1 0" range="-2.6 1.6"/>
          <joint name="shoulder_r_roll"  axis="1 0 0" range="-1.6 0.4"/>
          <joint name="shoulder_r_yaw"   axis="0 0 1" range="-1.5 1.5"/>
          <geom name="uar_geom" fromto="0 0 0 0 0 -0.28" size="0.045" mass="2.0"/>

          <body name="lower_arm_right" pos="0 0 -0.28">
            <joint name="elbow_r" axis="0 1 0" range="-2.4 0.0"/>
            <geom name="lar_geom" fromto="0 0 0 0 0 -0.25" size="0.038" mass="1.4"/>

            <body name="hand_right" pos="0 0 -0.25">
              <joint name="wrist_r_pitch" axis="0 1 0" range="-1.2 1.2"/>
              <joint name="wrist_r_roll"  axis="1 0 0" range="-1.2 1.2"/>
              <geom name="hand_geom" type="box" size="0.028 0.020 0.050" pos="0 0 -0.05"
                    mass="0.5" rgba="0.62 0.66 0.74 1"/>
              <!-- 0.105 m beyond the hand frame origin: the whole frames section rests on
                   this offset being real and non-zero. -->
              <site name="palm" pos="0 0 -0.105" size="0.012" rgba="0.90 0.30 0.20 1"/>
            </body>
          </body>
        </body>

        <!-- Two dofs that cannot move the right palm. Their Jacobian columns are exactly
             zero, which is the point. -->
        <body name="upper_arm_left" pos="0 0.19 0.36">
          <joint name="shoulder_l_pitch" axis="0 1 0" range="-2.6 1.6"/>
          <geom name="ual_geom" fromto="0 0 0 0 0 -0.28" size="0.045" mass="2.0"/>

          <body name="lower_arm_left" pos="0 0 -0.28">
            <joint name="elbow_l" axis="0 1 0" range="-2.4 0.0"/>
            <geom name="lal_geom" fromto="0 0 0 0 0 -0.25" size="0.038" mass="1.4"/>

            <body name="hand_left" pos="0 0 -0.25">
              <geom name="handl_geom" type="box" size="0.028 0.020 0.050" pos="0 0 -0.05"
                    mass="0.5" rgba="0.62 0.66 0.74 1"/>
              <site name="palm_left" pos="0 0 -0.105" size="0.012" rgba="0.30 0.50 0.80 1"/>
            </body>
          </body>
        </body>
      </body>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <keyframe>
    <!-- "ready": a generic working pose, well away from the singular home pose, used for the
         Jacobian and finite-difference sections. Nothing about it is special except that the
         arm is bent — the elbow at -0.85 rad keeps the smallest singular value of the
         translational Jacobian comfortably away from zero, so the finite-difference
         comparison measures discretisation error and nothing else. -->
    <key name="ready" qpos="0.0 0.10 -0.45 -0.25 0.0 -0.85 0.10 0.0 -0.30 -0.60"/>
  </keyframe>
</mujoco>
